# Local cuff replacement assessment

No geometry removed or replaced.

The integrated tunic/sleeve is closed manifold (zero boundary/nonmanifold edges). Horizontal connectivity cuts at z2.86,2.88,2.90,2.92,2.94 never isolate the cuff: each nearest component spans the torso/shoulders. There is no pre-existing detachable annular cuff or open wrist rim.

The previously audited anterior support defines1511 complete faces with a single166-vertex closed boundary loop (degree2 throughout). Thus replacing only this anterior surface patch while preserving its exact boundary is topologically feasible. It is a disk-like surface patch, not a verified complete sleeve collar. This topology does not justify removing surrounding hidden chest-side/circumferential geometry or inventing a full cuff opening.

A distinct replacement sleeve junction would require a newly delineated closed collar interface (including concealed side) and desired wrist opening, verified against real reference. Within the current safely audited anterior-only support, rebuilding a full collar is not robust; leaving the hidden original section and adding a new collar would be an overlap/remend rejected by the task. No such overlap or destructive cut was attempted.

Next viable scope: manually establish an anatomical cut boundary covering the full cuff, or explicitly reconstruct the whole forearm/sleeve region in an isolated copy with adequate reference. Do not infer broad torso-edit authorization from this technical assessment.
